## Hard rules

1. Edit only `{{SOURCE}}` and headers under `include/` or `src/` that it needs. Do NOT edit
   `configure.py`, anything in `config/`, `tools/`, `.github/`, `.claude/`, `AGENTS.md` or
   `CLAUDE.md`. Do not flip any object to `Matching`; a human does that.
2. Do not run git commands that change history or remotes (no commit, push, reset, rebase,
   checkout of other branches, stash). The harness commits for you. Do not use `gh`, `curl`
   or `wget`.
3. At most {{CYCLES}} build-diff cycles per function. Then stop on that function, leave a
   `// TODO:` that records what you tried and the remaining difference, and move on.
4. No fakematches: no dummy locals, no `goto`, no `__forceinline`, no invented layouts, no
   `extern` globals (see AGENTS.md "Globals"). If the residual is a c2 tie-break or an
   inline-budget problem you cannot fix with a same-bytes rewrite, leave it nonmatching with a TODO.
5. Never make a function you were not assigned worse. If an edit to a shared header changes
   other units, delete the affected objects and rebuild them (the `cl` rule has no header
   dependencies) or revert the header edit.
6. Use original names where the Mac symbols give them; follow the naming rules in AGENTS.md.
   Keep address comments in the exact AGENTS.md format.
7. Run `clang-format -i` on every C++ file you touched before you finish.
8. Do not run a bare `ninja` for the whole project and do not run `ninja baseline`; the
   harness runs the full verification after you finish.

